---
/*
  The sitewide bar. It states the business model, which is the thing a first-time
  visitor is least likely to guess: every feature works without paying, and a
  license is support rather than access.

  It used to be the founding-price bar — "$14.99 instead of $29.99 — 50% off".
  That was the right bar while buying was how you kept using the app. Now that it
  isn't, leading with a discount answers a question nobody asked and makes the
  cheaper thing sound like the point.

  No price here on purpose. The bar renders on all 54 pages including every docs
  page, where a number is least useful and most likely to go stale; the price
  lives on /, /download and /buy, all of which derive it from promos.ts. Dropping
  it also means this file cannot contradict what Polar charges.

  Renders unconditionally, unlike the offer bar it replaces, because the sentence
  is true whether or not an offer is running. That gives up the old "no bar to
  remember to take down" property — if it should ever be temporary again, gate it
  on a dated constant.
  price.

  Deliberately without a dismiss button: that needs client JS and per-visitor
  state, and a slim two-clause bar does not earn either.

  Styles are local rather than in global.css because the docs run on Starlight
  and never load that file. The marketing tokens get fallbacks for the same
  reason — inside the docs, `--orange-700` and friends do not exist.
*/
---

<div class="support-bar">
  <p>
    <strong>Free to use</strong>
    {/* Short enough that the docs form still fits one line at the narrowest the
        content column gets — 532px, around 832px viewport, where Starlight has
        just given the sidebar its width but the window is still small. "no
        trial" was in here and cost the line; nothing expires says it, and a
        trial nobody was offered is not worth the words. */}
    <span class="lede">— every feature, nothing expires</span>
    {/* First to go on a narrow screen and inside the docs column, where the bar
        gets ~690px rather than the window. The ask still survives there, in the
        link's own label — which is why the link says what it is for rather than
        "Get it". */}
    <span class="detail">· donations are what fund the work</span>
    <a href="/donate/">Donate</a>
  </p>
</div>

<style>
  .support-bar {
    background: var(--orange-700, #c2410c);
    color: #ffffff;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  .support-bar p {
    margin: 0 auto;
    max-width: var(--max-width, 72rem);
    padding: 0.5rem 1.5rem;
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0 0.4rem;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .support-bar strong {
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
  }

  .support-bar a {
    /* Without this the 6px flex gap left the sentence running straight into the
       link, reading as one phrase. */
    margin-inline-start: 0.35rem;
    color: #ffffff;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .support-bar a:hover {
    text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
  }

  /* Below this the full sentence runs to three lines and starts reading as a
     paragraph. The claim and the link survive; the optional-license clause is
     on /buy and in the bar on wider screens. */
  @media (max-width: 52rem) {
    .support-bar .detail {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .support-bar {
      font-size: 0.85rem;
    }

    .support-bar p {
      padding-inline: 1rem;
    }
  }
</style>
